# MASTER DEVELOPMENT PROMPT: ECOTWIN AI
## Environmental Risk Prediction and Decision Support System

Act as a senior full-stack engineer, machine-learning engineer, environmental data scientist, UI/UX designer, and software architect.

Build a complete, professional, production-quality academic project called **EcoTwin AI — Environmental Risk Prediction and Decision Support System**.

The application must be developed in Visual Studio Code as a fully functional, software-only environmental digital-twin prototype. It must combine real environmental datasets, locally trained machine-learning models, interactive environmental visualizations, scenario simulation, and evidence-based recommendations.

Do not build only a frontend mockup. Implement the frontend, backend, data pipeline, model training, model inference, database integration, validation, testing, and documentation.

---

# 1. PROJECT VISION

EcoTwin AI creates a virtual representation of a selected geographical environment using historical environmental data.

It allows users to:

- Analyze historical environmental conditions.
- Explore air-pollution patterns across supported locations.
- Train and evaluate machine-learning models.
- Predict selected environmental indicators.
- Visualize observations and predictions on interactive maps.
- Simulate hypothetical environmental scenarios.
- Compare baseline and modified scenarios.
- Receive rule-based, evidence-linked environmental recommendations.
- Export environmental analysis reports.

The first implementation must focus on air-quality and PM2.5 prediction. Design the architecture so vegetation analysis, temperature, rainfall, and water-quality modules can be added later.

This is an environmental science and AI/ML project, not a hardware or IoT project.

## Mandatory restrictions

1. Do not require physical sensors, Arduino, ESP32, Raspberry Pi, or any hardware integration.
2. Do not require paid AI APIs, cloud AI services, or API keys.
3. Do not use hardcoded predictions presented as real ML outputs.
4. Do not fabricate datasets, evaluation metrics, pollution readings, model accuracy, or environmental impact.
5. Do not display simulated values as live environmental measurements.
6. Do not stop after creating a static UI. Implement functional workflows.
7. Do not leave major modules as empty pages, TODO comments, or nonfunctional buttons.
8. Prefer free and open-source technologies.
9. Run the application locally on a standard Windows laptop.
10. If a dataset or external service is unavailable, provide a clear fallback using a bundled, documented sample dataset. Label sample data explicitly.

